{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat decisions/D028-kv-precision-protocol.md decisions/D029-c1-q8reuse-accepted.md decisions/D030-hybrid-split-rejected.md decisions/D031-split-diagnosis-proxy-scale.md decisions/D032-pap-and-planner-validation-protocol.md'","aggregated_output":"# D028 — KV-cache precision as a memory lever (protocol frozen before results)\nQuestion: at long context, how much GPU memory does KV quantization save on Granite mixE4K, at what quality and speed cost? KV/token f16 = 24 layers x 2 x 8 kv-heads x 64 x 2 B = 48 KiB -> 1.5 GiB at 32k ctx (> mixE4K weights 779 MiB).\nImage: llamacpp-b0 (unpatched 31288c9c6). Tests lab/tests/kv1.json: decode/prefill at -c 32768 with the 1560-token prose prompt (-n 96) for KV f16/q8_0/q4_0 with -fa on, plus f16 -fa off control; 3 alternating rounds; PPL wikitext -c 4096 --chunks 6 per KV type.\nAccept a KV type as \"same accuracy\" if PPL within +1% of f16 (-fa on) AND greedy text identical or (if not) KLD check pending; report GPU delta MiB (nvidia-smi), prefill & decode t/s medians. Classification: representation (KV) trade-off — not an engine code gain.\nRESULT (job job-787f02f737, 7/15 clean; host monitor clean):\n- Quantized KV (q8_0, q4_0; -fa on) ABORTS at model load: ggml-impl.h:330 hash-set \"fatal error\" (SIGSEGV 139), in our b0 build AND the upstream ghcr full-cuda image at the same commit, with fit on or off, at 4k and 32k ctx -> engine defect in llama.cpp 31288c9c6 for this model/config, not our build. Delegated root cause: T-KVB. KV precision lever UNTESTED, not \"rejected\".\n- Flash attention alone (f16 KV, -c 32768, 1560-token prompt): GPU delta 2762 MiB (-fa on) vs 3746 MiB (-fa off) = -984 MiB; decode 627.2 vs 570.2 t/s (+10%), prefill 7001 vs 5865 t/s (+19%); text differs (96310158 vs f28de009; FA changes reduction order). PPL -c 4096 f16 FA 7.5385 (no non-FA PPL run -> quality equivalence of FA not measured here).\nClassification: memory-fit + speed observation for an existing engine option; factory rule candidate: \"enable flash attention for long context (removes the O(ctx) KQ buffer)\". 3 samples (gen), 1 (ppl).\n# D029 — C1 q8_reuse ACCEPTED: first full-engine computation-preserving gain (+2.1% Q8_0 decode), x86-main\nProtocol D026 (frozen, commit 474be3b before results). Job job-34c09bec98 (image llamacpp-q8reuse2, run_ref sha256:bd78b473..., config 111ddae8...; patch 9943dee4...), same binary GGML_CUDA_Q8_REUSE=0 vs 1, 2 ABBA cycles, 36/36 clean, host monitor 38 samples no compiler, load 2.7-5.0.\nllama-bench tg128 median t/s [range]: Q8_0 OFF 791.3 [789-792] ON 807.6 [806-808] = +2.06% (criterion >= +2%: MET, ranges disjoint). mixE4K OFF 702.9 ON 715.4 = +1.78% (below 2% -> mixE4K track \"consistent but sub-threshold\").\nllama-completion decode (chat, 160 tok): Q8_0 758.4 -> 777.0 (+2.45%); mixE4K 677.6 -> 691.2 (+2.0%). Prefill unchanged within noise.\nCorrectness: CUDA test-backend-ops MUL_MAT 2445/2445 OK; greedy text sha identical base/OFF/ON (Q8_0 65894776, mixE4K 950b46b1); PPL identical (Q8_0 9.653, mixE4K 9.9806, -ub 1).\nClassification: full-engine code gain, computation-preserving, discrete RTX 5070 Ti, Granite, batch-1 decode. Small: it's a launch/redundancy cleanup (D011 predicted 3-4.5%). Not memory-reducing; stacks with every memory technique. Component CC: cc/q8_reuse (integration: env switch, CUDA mmvq path, applies to 31288c9c6).\n# D030 — T-R hybrid expert split REJECTED as implemented (slower than static layer offload at equal GPU MiB)\nProtocol D027 (+amendment 1). Job job-d1fb4e9879 (image llamacpp-hsplit1 run_ref sha256:6e91ace0...), same binary for all, 27/27 clean, 3 alternating rounds.\nDecode median t/s (GPU delta MiB): n18: static 149.8 (902) | static+flags 144.5 | split 125.3 (910) = -16%.  n12: 189.8 | 198.8 | split 160.8 (1072) = -19%.  n6: 309.1 | 311.5 | split 211.9 (1234) = -32%. Prefill also lower (split 211-252 vs static 269-322).\nQuality gate not reached (rejected on speed first); CUDA unit-test mismatch still open (amendment 1).\nKey observation: the deficit GROWS as more experts are hot (n18 -> n6), so CPU expert compute is not the bottleneck; candidates: (1) per-slot GPU dispatch (patch issues one MUL_MAT_ID per selected slot to avoid duplicate null IDs in mmid.cu -> up to 8x kernels per projection), (2) 24 GPU<->CPU handoffs/token (every layer has a cold part) vs K for static, (3) router-order GET_ROWS/transposes. Next discriminating experiment: nsys timeline split-n12 vs static-n12 (kernel count/token, sync count, CPU-busy intervals).\nLesson (methodology): single-layer harness predicted ~330 t/s at ~897 MiB (D019); the integrated graph pays per-layer fixed costs the harness did not model. Harness wins are hypotheses until integrated (prompt §13 confirmed).\n# D031 — Why the hybrid split loses on Granite (nsys, T-PROF) and the proxy-scale bias it reveals\nEvidence: artifacts/codexPROF/ANALYSIS.md, cc/nsys_decode/analyze_nsys.py (new profiling CC), artifacts/x4-hs/prof/{split,static}-n12.sqlite (graph-level, same binary, 154 decode tokens after 5 warmup).\nPer token: static n12 5.35 ms (13 graph launches, 110 syncs, GPU busy 1.06 ms = GPU idle ~80%); split n12 7.38 ms (25 graph launches, 194 syncs, GPU busy 2.55 ms). Extra 2.03 ms = 1.45 ms extra GPU graph work (per-slot hot dispatch: 1535 vs 583 kernels in first decode) + 0.37 ms exposed sync/transfers + 0.21 ms host. CUDA graphs ARE reused (no capture loss).\nInterpretation (orchestrator): at batch 1 on this host, decode is dominated by per-layer CPU<->GPU handoff latency, not by expert bytes or GPU compute. The split computes FEWER CPU experts per token (~52 vs 96 at ~73% hot coverage) yet is slower because it pays the handoff in 24 layers instead of 12. T-PROF's best redesign (batched CUDA dispatch with skip IDs, -0.7..-1.1 ms) would only reach ~parity with static.\nTransferability (key factory lesson): Granite experts are 0.84 MiB; the target (~500 GB MoE) has experts ~10-50x larger, so per-layer fixed costs amortize and expert-granular placement (hot GPU / cold CPU, Strata-style) should regain its advantage. Granite-only results systematically favour layer-granular placement. => The factory needs a second proxy that genuinely exceeds VRAM with larger experts before deciding placement granularity; Granite results on placement are labelled \"small-expert regime\".\nMethodology rule: report per-layer fixed cost (handoff latency) alongside per-expert cost; predict crossover expert size = fixed_cost / per_byte_cost before choosing granularity.\n# D032 — Protocols frozen before results: (A) placement-aware expert precision (PAP, T-PAP) and (B) planner prediction validation (T-PL)\n(A) Hypothesis: CPU-resident expert part is DRAM-bound (~26 GB/s); lower-bit experts ONLY in the first K (CPU) layers speed decode ~ by bytes saved and cut host RAM, while KLD rises only modestly; imatrix (wikitext TRAIN 100x512, sha 106f834b) reduces low-bit KLD. Models: artifacts/codexPAP/MANIFEST.json. Image llamacpp-b0 (same as D022). Tests lab/tests/pap1.json: 13 decode configs x 3 alternating rounds (pap{K}_{q3,q2} at --n-cpu-moe K; controls mixE4K and mix4_imat at the same K; pap24_q3_noimat), then KLD 20x512 vs bf16.kl for 9 models.\nAccept PAP{K}_{q} as a \"better memory/speed point\" if decode median >= +5% vs mixE4K at the same K AND KLD <= 0.08 (2x mixE4K's 0.040; D013 Q4_K_M uniform is 0.078 at higher memory). imatrix effect = KLD(pap24_q3) vs KLD(pap24_q3_noimat). Classification: representation trade-off (NOT computation-preserving).\n(B) Planner (cc/moe_planner, calibrated on D022) predicted for 900 MiB @ ctx 4096: n19..n23 mixE4K with gpu_mib/decode predictions in artifacts/codexPL/plan-900.json. Tests lab/tests/plan1.json (timing x3 alternating + quality). Score: |pred-meas|/meas for GPU MiB and decode; planner \"validated in range\" if decode error <= 16% (its own LOO bound) and GPU MiB within budget for all 5.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
